8ca3f5f85816bfd35ffd190001128eb216259f8ba34c3190d280010b29.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-109971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/assets/301b3a5f659be1efaa44d359d36acb055d45181b4d4e27b63f9fba7136e34867.png)\n\nWhich of the following systems of inequalities defines the shaded region shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-109971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Consider the line $y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *below* the line, the inequality is a \"less than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-2.$ Since the line is solid, the inequality is an \"or equal to\" inequality. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"gretaer than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\ge -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le \\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-109971" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/ref