" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny\\le x+2.\n$$\n\n    \n\nNow consider the line $y=-x+2.$ Since the line is dashed, the inequality is strict. Also, since the shaded region lies *above* the line, the inequality is a \"greater than\" inequality. So the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny>-x+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the system of inequalities that defines the shaded region is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-90327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a65358a8b5ef7055c4952abcc1fce216480695a123f917818b4365ffd9c265f4"} {"question" "q-90327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-90327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y<x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y>x+2 \\\\ y\\le -x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge x+2 \\\\ y<-x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y\\ge -x+2\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-90327" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}y\\le x+2 \\\\ y>-x+2\\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-25194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\ge 3x \\\\ y>\\frac{1}{4}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-25194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To graph the first inequality $y\\ge 3x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=3x.$\n\n- Since we have a \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *above* the line.\n\nTo graph the second inequality $y>\\frac{1}{4}x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=\\frac{1}{4}x.$\n\n- Since we have a strict inequality, the line itself is *not* included in the solution, so the line is dashed.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than*  those on the line, so we shade *above* the line.\n\nThe intersection of the solutions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/assets/15608503fc9aa3ffe20cd649cf2d0adab657a09961811c206a7bce16a34c9c2f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996965/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f52905f69ca98169e3e7006f25135a2fc71e6ea64c85d5f5f04bfca92ce119b"} {"question" "q-25194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/d